$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: 4.0 Characterization data 2.0 Aldehyde
acetalization catalyzed by 22 -general procedure A 20 mL reaction vial
containing a stirring bar was charged with 22 (0.05 mmol), fitted with a
septum and flushed with argon. The appropriate aldehyde (0.50 mmol) was
added followed by dry methanol (1.25 mL, 0.38 M) via syringe. The
solution was then stirred at room temperature for 24 h. When conversion
was judged to be either complete or >95% (by 1H-NMR spectroscopic
analysis) the reaction was quenched with PhNHNH2 and the solvent was
removed in vacuo. The crude product was then purified by
flash-chromatography. 4.0 Characterization data Benzaldehyde dimethyl
acetal (3, Table 2, entry 1) Phenyl-1,3-dioxane (41, Table 3, entry 4)
The desired dioxane was obtained following the general procedure using
catalyst 22 (23.1 mg, 0.06 mmol), 1,3-propanediol (100 μL, 1.38 mmol) and
benzaldehyde (128 μL, 1.26 mmol). After purification of the crude
material by flash chromatography the product 41 was obtained as a pale
yellow liquid (183 mg, 88%). 1H NMR (CDCl3) δ 1.44-1.48 (m, 1H),
2.18-2.30 (m, 1H), 4.00 (ddd, 2H, J = 14.0, 12.3, 1.7 Hz), 4.28 (ddd, 2H,
J = 11.7, 5.3, 1.2 Hz), 5.51 (s, 1H), 7.31-7.39 (m, 3H), 7.48-7.50 (m,
2H). 13C NMR (CDCl3) δ 25.3, 67.0, 101.2, 125.6, 127.8, 128.4, 138.4.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM alternative preparation gave lower yield, recyclable catalyst
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Unexpected catalysis: aprotic pyridinium ions as active and recyclable
Bronsted acid catalysts in protic media
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Procuranti, Barbara; Connon, Stephen J.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Letters (2008), 10(21), 4935-4938

